   "texts": {
    "profile": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Inputs, Light Capture, and Outputs\n\nINTRO: Nothing here is scored; a wrong answer only shows what to look at next. This level goes straight to the new part: what photosynthesis uses, where light is captured, and what is released.\n\nSECTION 1 - The core rule: inputs become glucose\nPhotosynthesis is how plants make glucose, a sugar, from carbon dioxide and water. They use light energy from the sun to drive the reaction, and oxygen is released as a by-product. The word equation is carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. Takeaway: the inputs are carbon dioxide, water, and light; the outputs are glucose and oxygen.\nEXAMPLE: Video game crafting: carbon dioxide and water are the ingredients, light is the energy bar, glucose is the item you craft, and oxygen is the extra item that drops. Try it: Which two raw materials does a plant use to make glucose? Answer: carbon dioxide and water.\n\nSECTION 2 - Chlorophyll captures the light\nChlorophyll is the green pigment that absorbs light. It sits inside chloroplasts, which are structures in leaf cells. Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves because leaves contain most of the chloroplasts. Takeaway: chlorophyll in chloroplasts captures the light, mostly in leaves.\nEXAMPLE: Drawing comics: think of a leaf as the page, a chloroplast as the studio on that page, and chlorophyll as the light-sensitive ink that takes in the light. Try it: What pigment absorbs light, and where is it found? Answer: chlorophyll, in chloroplasts.\n\nSECTION 3 - Oxygen is the by-product\nOxygen is released as a by-product; it is not the glucose the plant made. Because photosynthesis happens mainly in leaves, most oxygen release also happens there. The glucose can be used for growth or stored as starch. Takeaway: oxygen leaves the plant as a by-product, while glucose is the useful product.\nEXAMPLE: Basketball: glucose is the basket your team scores, while oxygen is the assist that does not count as the score. Try it: Is oxygen the plant's food or a by-product? Answer: a by-product.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A leaf is in sunlight and has 6 carbon dioxide molecules and 6 water molecules available. Use the balanced summary to find the products and the number of oxygen molecules released.\n  1. Write the inputs from the problem: 6CO2 and 6H2O.\n  2. Add the energy source: light energy from the sun drives the reaction.\n  3. Apply the balanced summary: 6CO2 + 6H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6O2. The products are one glucose molecule and six oxygen molecules.\n  4. Check the count: 6 carbon, 12 hydrogen, and 18 oxygen atoms appear on each side, so the equation balances.\n  ANSWER: Products: glucose (C6H12O6) and oxygen (6O2); 6 oxygen molecules are released.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants get their glucose from soil, so photosynthesis just moves sugar up from the roots. Why: soil supplies water and minerals, not glucose; the glucose is made in the leaves from carbon dioxide and water using light energy. Right: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen.\n\nSUMMARY: Photosynthesis: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. Chlorophyll in chloroplasts absorbs the light, the process happens mainly in leaves, and oxygen is released as a by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which statement correctly describes photosynthesis? [choices: Plants take in glucose from the soil and release carbon dioxide. | Plants make glucose from carbon dioxide and water using light energy. | Plants make oxygen from glucose and release chlorophyll. | Plants use chlorophyll as a by-product and store it in roots.] -> Plants make glucose from carbon dioxide and water using light energy.\n2. A leaf has carbon dioxide, water, and chlorophyll, but it is kept in complete darkness. Can it make glucose? Answer yes or no, and give the reason. [typed answer] -> No. Light energy is required; chlorophyll can absorb light, but in darkness there is no light for it to absorb, so photosynthesis cannot run.",
    "mirror": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Plants Making Sugar from Light\n\nINTRO: Photosynthesis is how plants make their own food. They use light energy to turn two materials into a sugar called glucose. You will see the recipe, where it happens, and what comes out.\n\nSECTION 1 - The Photosynthesis Recipe\nPhotosynthesis is the process plants use to make glucose, a sugar that is plant food. The plant takes in carbon dioxide, a gas from the air, and water from the soil. These two materials are the inputs, the starting stuff. Light energy from the sun powers the change. The rule is: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose.\nEXAMPLE: Example: A bean plant in sunlight takes carbon dioxide from the air through its leaves and water from the soil through its roots. Light energy powers the change, and the plant makes glucose. Like a basketball team turning passes and effort into points, the plant turns its inputs plus light into sugar.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where It Happens: Leaves and Chloroplasts\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves. Leaves contain chloroplasts, which are tiny green parts inside plant cells. Chloroplasts hold chlorophyll, the green substance that absorbs light. Absorb means to take in. Chlorophyll captures light energy so the plant can use it.\nEXAMPLE: Example: A plant sits in a dark cupboard. Step 1: photosynthesis needs light energy. Step 2: chlorophyll must absorb that light. Step 3: in the dark, there is no light for chlorophyll to absorb. Finish the last step: so the plant ___. Like a video game crafting station that needs power, the leaf needs chlorophyll to catch light.\n\nSECTION 3 - What Comes Out: Oxygen and Sugar\nGlucose is the sugar the plant uses for energy and to build new plant parts. Oxygen is released as a by-product, which means a gas given off that is not the main product. The oxygen goes into the air. Animals and people breathe that oxygen.\nEXAMPLE: Example: A plant in a sunny window makes glucose and gives off oxygen. Try it: Name the gas a plant releases as a by-product. Answer: oxygen.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A tomato plant has water in its soil and carbon dioxide in the air around its leaves. Sunlight is shining on it. What does the plant make, and what does it release?\n  1. Find the inputs. The plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil. These are the starting materials.\n  2. Find the energy source. Sunlight supplies light energy. Without light, the recipe cannot run.\n  3. Find where it happens. The leaves contain chloroplasts, and chlorophyll inside them absorbs the light energy.\n  4. Find the main product. The plant uses carbon dioxide, water, and light energy to make glucose, a sugar.\n  5. Find the by-product. Oxygen is released as a by-product and goes into the air.\n  ANSWER: The plant makes glucose and releases oxygen.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants make glucose from oxygen and water. Why: oxygen is the by-product that is released, not an input; the gas taken in is carbon dioxide. Right: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose, and oxygen is released as a by-product.\nWrong: Chlorophyll is the tiny part of the leaf that holds the chloroplasts. Why: this swaps the two names; chloroplasts are the tiny parts, and chlorophyll is the green substance inside them that absorbs light. Right: chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, and chlorophyll absorbs light energy.\nWrong: Photosynthesis happens mainly in the roots. Why: roots take in water, but they do not have the green chloroplasts that absorb light; leaves do. Right: photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n\nSUMMARY: Photosynthesis: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. It happens mainly in leaves, where chlorophyll in chloroplasts absorbs light. Glucose is the plant's sugar, and oxygen is released as a by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which gas do plants release as a by-product of photosynthesis? (Hint: it is the gas people breathe in.) [choices: Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Nitrogen | Hydrogen] -> Oxygen\n2. What is the green substance inside chloroplasts that absorbs light energy? (Hint: it is also why leaves look green.) [typed answer] -> Chlorophyll\n3. A learner writes: 'Plants make glucose from oxygen and water.' What is the mistake? (Hint: check which gas is an input.) [choices: Oxygen is a by-product, not an input; the input gas is carbon dioxide. | Water is not used in photosynthesis. | Glucose is a by-product, not the main product. | Plants make glucose from sunlight alone, with no gases.] -> Oxygen is a by-product, not an input; the input gas is carbon dioxide."
   }
